  "answer": "Mendeleev’s periodic table had several limitations. One major limitation was the presence of anomalous pairs of elements, where elements with higher atomic weights were placed before those with lower atomic weights. For example, tellurium (Te) with an atomic weight of 127.6 was placed before iodine (I) with an atomic weight of 126.9. Mendeleev accepted these minor inversions because they helped place elements in the correct groups. Another limitation was the placement of dissimilar elements in the same group, such as alkali metals (like Li, Na, K) and coinage metals (like Cu, Ag, Au), which have very different properties. Additionally, the table did not account for the discovery of new elements, and Mendeleev had to leave blank spaces for them. He predicted the properties of these elements, which were later confirmed upon their discovery. However, the modern periodic table addresses these limitations by arranging elements based on their atomic numbers rather than atomic weights. This change eliminates the problem of anomalous series and ensures that elements with similar properties are grouped together. The modern periodic table also provides a more accurate representation of the electronic configurations of elements, which explains their chemical and physical properties more effectively.",
